# Python's entry in the acceptance suite — the FIRST instance of D032 §5 B3.
#
# This file is data. `spikes/binding_suite.sh` reads it and contains no
# language name of its own; everything Python-specific about Python's
# acceptance is here or in `spikes/bindings/python/`.
#
# ── What a row is / 행의 뜻 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   cell   <direction> <peer> <command>   clause 1, measured in that cell
#   waits  <direction> <peer> <reason>    a cell nobody supplies, with a real
#                                         reason; the SKIPPED itself is printed
#                                         whether or not this row exists
#   clause <name>      <command>          clauses 3/4/5 — no peer, no wire
#
# A `cell` command runs from the repository root and answers with its EXIT
# STATUS: 0 ok, 1 red, 2 its fixture is absent. On stdout it may report, tab
# separated:
#
#   observed<TAB>giop=<v><TAB>order=<o>            read off §15.4.1's flag byte
#   claimed<TAB>giop=<v><TAB>order=<o><TAB><why>   asserted, NOT read
#   note<TAB><text>
#
# Only `observed` counts toward clause 2. That is the distinction the whole
# suite turns on: `claimed` is what a cell says when the order is right but
# nobody looked, and printing it as `observed` would be exactly the "green while
# measuring nothing" class in a new coat.
#
# ── Why these five cells and not six / 다섯 칸인 이유 ─────────────────────────
#
# The grid is (direction × peer) = 2 × 3. Five have runners. The sixth —
# client × jacorb — is the one D030 §3.1 already names in prose as the gap, and
# it is stated here as a `waits` row so the suite prints it every run instead of
# it living in a table nobody executes.
#
# *이 파일은 데이터다. 격자는 (방향 × 피어)이고 여섯 칸 중 다섯에 러너가 있다.
# 빠진 하나는 D030 §3.1이 이미 산문으로 지목한 바로 그 구멍이며, 여기서는 매 실행마다
# 인쇄되는 `waits` 행이다.*

# ── clause 1, over the grid ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The harness group `Python client target`'s first leg, unchanged: generated
# Python invokes a C++ target holding only a reference, with no Rust stub in the
# path. The adapter exists because the leg needs the omniORB fixture started and
# stopped around it, not because Python needs a special case.
cell	client	omniorb	spikes/bindings/python/client-omniorb.sh

# The cross-implementation sweep. It is in the grid because it is clause 1's
# measurement for this cell — every operation of a contract set called and
# answered — and it reports NO wire observation at all, which is the point: it
# is a value-level round trip through `_rt.Loopback` with no socket in it. A
# suite that counted this as "both byte orders" would be lying twice over.
cell	client	self	cargo test -q -p orbweaver-gen --test python_target

# omniORB's own Python client drives the Python servant behind our ORB.
cell	servant	omniorb	spikes/bindings/python/servant-omniorb.sh

# JacORB drives the same servant. The strongest cell in the grid and the only
# one anywhere that reads a foreign peer's byte order out of the flag byte.
cell	servant	jacorb	spikes/bindings/python/servant-jacorb.sh

# Our generated Rust client against the Python servant, plus the seam's own
# 19 calls × 3 versions × 2 orders comparison against a Rust servant. Both ends
# ours, so it can never satisfy clause 6 — which is why `peer` is an axis.
cell	servant	self	cargo test -q -p orbweaver-gen --test python_servant

# The gap, stated so it is printed rather than remembered.
cell	client	jacorb	spikes/bindings/python/client-jacorb.sh

# ── clauses 3, 4 and 5 — language-scoped, no peer and no wire ────────────────

# Clause 3. The refusal a generated stub gives for a form the wire cannot carry
# must be EQUAL to the published head, not similar — held by computing the
# expected text from the same function rather than by a literal.
clause	refusals-equal-the-published-heads	cargo test -q -p orbweaver-gen --test python_target -- a_peer_fed_form_reads_as_a_description_and_refuses_as_a_value

# Clause 4, in two halves: the exception a reply names arrives as itself, and a
# repository id is spelled by the emitter's own naming function at every segment.
clause	an-exception-arrives-as-what-the-reply-named	cargo test -q -p orbweaver-gen --test python_target -- a_generated_stub_raises_what_the_reply_names
clause	a-stub-is-found-by-the-name-the-emitter-gave-it	cargo test -q -p orbweaver-gen --test python_target -- a_stub_is_looked_up_by_the_name_the_emitter_gave_it

# Clause 5. `--language` is passed through rather than the binary knowing which
# language it is for; `binding-words` looks the target up in
# `orbweaver_gen::targets::TARGETS`.
clause	keyword-escaping-is-executed	cargo run -q -p orbweaver-gen --bin binding-words -- --language python --allow spikes/bindings/keywords-not-executed.tsv --idl corpus/golden/28-target-keywords.idl
